This invention relates to a process for producing succinic acid that includes the following stages:

—introducing an aqueous solution of magnesium succinate into an acidification stage, where hydrogen chloride is added to acidify the solution, yielding an aqueous mixture containing both succinic acid and magnesium chloride;

—passing the resulting succinic acid/magnesium chloride solution from the acidification stage through an activated carbon treatment stage;

—performing a precipitation stage on the aqueous mixture (containing succinic acid and magnesium chloride) that emerges from the activated carbon treatment, which yields solid succinic acid along with a separate magnesium chloride solution;

—separating the solid succinic acid from the remaining magnesium chloride solution;

—heating the magnesium chloride solution to temperatures of 300° C. or above to thermally decompose it, converting the magnesium chloride into magnesium oxide and hydrogen chloride gas; and

—returning the hydrogen chloride produced during this thermal decomposition back to the initial acidification stage for reuse.

Research has demonstrated that this particular method produces succinic acid crystals exhibiting superior characteristics compared to an equivalent process that omits the activated carbon treatment step.
